**Step 4: Confirm the `atoi` out-of-bounds read.**
The `atoi` function does not perform bounds checking. It expects a null-terminated string. If `colon + 1` (or `p + 2` in the IPv6 case) points to the very end of the `str` buffer (i.e., `str + str_len`), then `atoi` will attempt to read past the end of the buffer to find a null terminator or non-digit character. This is an out-of-bounds read.

**Example for IPv4 case:**
Input: `str = "127.0.0.1:"`, `str_len = 10`
- `colon` will point to the last character, `:`.
- `colon + 1` will point to `str + 10`, which is one byte past the end of the valid `str` buffer.
- `atoi(colon + 1)` will read from `str + 10`, `str + 11`, etc., until it finds a null terminator or a non-digit character. This is an out-of-bounds read.

**Example for IPv6 case:**
Input: `str = "[::]:"`, `str_len = 5`
